            {
               "text": "<p style=\"font-style: italic;\" id=\"htChinaZhongguo\"></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text continues the previous one (<htcite0>), so we recommend reading it first.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d06e24cb-52e8-4ff8-ae37-12052c91b187",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Limiting",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nTo simplify our daily lives, we usually give names to people, animals, objects—and also to numbers (<htcite0>). Besides the name itself, we often use nicknames that shorten it. The habit is so natural, after a lifetime of it, that we rarely notice when a simplification completely changes what something means.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d06e24cb-52e8-4ff8-ae37-12052c91b187",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Limiting",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: It Could Be Like That, But It Isn't</b><br /><br />Can you think of any nickname or simplification that you use and that, in reality, completely changes the understanding of something?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_names",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Naming\n\nSometimes, because something resembles what we know or has similar functions, we are tempted to name it in a way that ends up misrepresenting it. Let's look at some examples.\n\n#### Sacred Mountains\n\nOne example we have already explored is the *Witz*—the Maya sacred mountains of America (*Abya Yala*) (**Figure 1**)—which some people call “pyramids”, even though they are not (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "5c61211a-87e4-45ee-9d27-739625a7971c",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Together we are strong",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"img4\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img4', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Sacred mountain at Xunantunich, Belize. Photograph taken on <htdate0>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2016",
                     "month": "7",
                     "day": "22"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: What it seems like</b><br /><br />Observe Figure 1 carefully. Does it remind you of a pyramid?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "If your answer was yes, that is not a problem: it only means that the definition of a pyramid has not crossed your path yet. Let's look at the definition (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1aae32d7-8b8a-49d9-b136-f859e24a53e6",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "Pirámide",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><ul class=\"citation\"> <li>\"<i>A pyramid is a geometric figure with a closed base and triangular faces that converge at a single point at the top, known as the apex.</i>\"</li></ul></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Now that we have the definition of a pyramid—and the **triangular** lateral faces it requires, as in **Figure 2**—we can revisit **Reflection 2**.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table class=\"book_navigation\"><tr><td>Pyramid with a Square Base</td><td>Pyramid with a Pentagonal Base</td></tr><tr><td><img id=\"img2\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img2', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td><td><img id=\"img3\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('img3', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/></td></tr><tr><td colspan=\"2\" class=\"desc\"><b>Figure 2</b>: Examples of pyramids illustrating the mathematical definition.</td></tr></table></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 3: Is it really a pyramid?</b><br /><br />Are there triangular faces on the <i>Witz</i> in <b>Figure 1</b>?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Different Letters\n\nMistakes are not limited to geometric figures. Another example, rooted in culture, is reading the characters of China (Zhongguo 中國) (**Figure 3**) as if they were Western letters, ignoring how complex their structure is (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "5c7398a3-7931-4afc-af5e-b505756e8963",
                     "text": "Dai, R., Liu, C. & Xiao, B.",
                     "page": "pp. 2 - 5",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2007",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgChinese\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgChinese', '0%')\" class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 3</b>: Arrowhead made of bone containing an inscription. © Trustees of the British Museum (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 0,
                     "uuid": "874a8d48-a892-4ad3-8bc4-b34190bd4eba",
                     "text": "The British Museum",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "30"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### What if it was an approximation?\n\nIt is tempting to say that, both for the pyramids and for the Chinese characters, we were only making an approximation. In both cases, however, what happened was a misinterpretation.\n\nBeyond these mistakes, there is another common one that appears when we round off or simplify concepts—as we will see next.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_decimal_name",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Names and Abbreviations\n\nJust as we group numbers into families—units, tens, hundreds, thousands (<htcite0>)—we also give names to the numbers we count with, and we abbreviate those names, as **Table 1** shows:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "d06e24cb-52e8-4ff8-ae37-12052c91b187",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Limiting",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<style>.localtablename {width: 60%;display: block;overflow-x: auto;max-width: 100%;margin-left: auto;margin-right: auto;text-align: center;border-collapse: collapse;} .localtablename th {border-collapse: collapse;background-color:lightgrey;} .localtablename td:nth-child(-n+1) {background-color:lightblue;}</style><p><table class=\"localtablename\"><tr><th>Number</th><th>Family</th><th>Prefix</th></tr> <tr><td>1,000,000,000,000,000,000</td><td>Quintillion</td><td>Exa</td> </tr> <tr><td>1,000,000,000,000,000</td><td>Quadrillion</td><td>Peta</td> </tr>  <tr><td>1,000,000,000,000</td><td>Trillion</td><td>Tera</td> </tr> <tr><td>1,000,000,000</td><td>Billion</td><td>Giga</td> </tr> <tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>Million</td><td>Mega</td> </tr> <tr><td>1,000</td><td>Thousand</td><td>Kilo</td> </tr><tr><td>1</td><td>Units</td><td>&nbsp;</td> </tr> <tr><td>0.1</td><td>Tenths</td><td>Deci</td> </tr> <tr><td>0.01</td><td>Hundredths</td><td>Centi</td> </tr> <tr><td>0.001</td><td>Thousandths</td><td>Milli</td> </tr> <tr><td>0.000001</td><td>Millionths</td><td>Micro</td> </tr> <tr><td>0.000000001</td><td>Billionths</td><td>Nano</td> </tr> <tr><td>0.000000000001</td><td>Trillionths</td><td>Pico</td> </tr> </table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: Names assigned to numbers.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "**Table 1** shows only some of the names we give to numbers; numbers are not limited to the examples shown. This brings us back to the question of numerical limits (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Natural limits",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 4: To infinity…</b><br /><br />If there is no end to decimal numbers, does that mean there are infinite numbers between 0 and 1?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_pi",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### To Be or Not to Be\n\nWhen we buy 1 kilogram of fruit, we are saying the same thing as buying 1000 grams of that fruit. In this way, the prefix from Table 1 helps us express how much we are buying. We also often hear people ask for “1 kilo”, using the prefix to stand for the 1000 grams they want.\n\nAbbreviations like these tempt us to generalize them to other situations—but that can lead to errors. In mathematics, for example, we define the constant pi (π) as the circumference—**C**, the length of the circle if we cut it and stretch it out flat—divided by its diameter—**d**, the width of the circle, which never changes—as shown in **Formula 1** (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a56b7c6c-93ce-4e7f-bf5e-781e4597485d",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"unitaryCircle\" id=\"circtri0\"><style>.circle-draw circle, .circle-draw line {stroke: #71a6d2; stroke-width: 4; stroke-linecap: round; fill: none;} .circle-draw circle { stroke-dasharray: 942; stroke-dashoffset: 942; animation: drawCircle 2s linear forwards;} .radius-line { transform-origin: 300px 150px; animation: rotateRadius 2s linear forwards;} .radius-45 { opacity: 0; animation: showRadius45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .radius-30{ opacity: 0; animation: showRadius30 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2s; } .vertical45 { opacity: 0; animation: showVertical45 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.5s; } .atlasTextRadius { opacity: 0; animation: atlasTextRadiusMov 0.3s ease-out forwards; animation-delay: 2.8s; }</style><style>@keyframes drawCircle { to {stroke-dashoffset: 0; } } @keyframes rotateRadius { from { transform: rotate(0deg); } to   { transform: rotate(-360deg); } } @keyframes showRadius30 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } } @keyframes showRadius45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes showVertical45 { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }  @keyframes atlasTextRadiusMov { from { opacity: 0; } to { opacity: 1; } }</style><div class=\"atlasCircleContainer\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 600 300\" class=\"circle-draw\" id=\"svgcirctri0\"> <circle cx=\"300\" cy=\"150\" r=\"148\" transform=\"scale(-1 1) translate(-600 0)  rotate(180 300 150)\"></circle>  <line class=\"radius-line\" x1=\"300\" y1=\"148\" x2=\"448\" y2=\"148\"></line>  <line class=\"vertical45\" x1=\"154\" y1=\"148\" x2=\"300\" y2=\"148\"></line> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"300\" y=\"168\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">d</text> <text class=\"atlasTextRadius\" x=\"407\" y=\"45\" font-size=\"1.3em\" font-weight=\"bold\">C</text> </svg></div></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Graph 1</b>: Circle with circumference C and diameter d.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math displaystyle=\"false\" mathsize=\"1.5em\"><mn>π</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>C</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>d</mn></mrow></mfrac></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: Definition of the constant π, with the values shown in Graph 1.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The result of **Formula 1** for any circle is given by **Calculation 1** (<htcite0>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "a56b7c6c-93ce-4e7f-bf5e-781e4597485d",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p><math displaystyle=\"false\" mathsize=\"1.5em\"><mn>π</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>C</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>d</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>3.141592653589793238462643383279502884197…</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Calculation of the value of π.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Note that, although the number has many digits after the decimal point—the digits we call decimals—the “…” at the end tells us there are still more.\n\nIn this situation it is tempting to do what we do with words and abbreviate, as with kilogram and kilo. But since pi (π) is a constant fully defined by **Formula 1**, cutting off digits after the decimal point goes beyond simplifying or rounding: it means we are no longer talking about pi (π).\n\nSo remember: if you use pi (π) as “3.14,” you are no longer working with π at all, but with the number 3.14 that stands in for it.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_conclusion",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Conclusion\n\nIn this text, we saw that between two numbers we use for counting, such as 0 and 1, there are infinitely many numbers (<htcite0>).\n\nWe also saw that although we abbreviate in everyday life, in some cases an abbreviation completely changes what something means—as with the constant pi (π).\n\nFinally, we learned that the digits after the decimal point are called decimals, and that takes us to one final reflection:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "18a901e4-3da3-45d8-a239-fb551e78698e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Natural limits",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 5: More Names</b><br /><br />How can we express the relationship between the numbers we count and the numbers that lie between them?</div></div></center>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "The answer to this reflection will be presented in the next text.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
   ],
   "exercise_v2": [
      {
         "question": "Is any construction—human or natural—that begins with a large base and ends at a point at the top a pyramid?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "No",
         "additionalInfo": "For a construction to be a pyramid, its lateral faces must be triangular and meet at a point at the top."
      },
      {
         "question": "Does pi (π) have a value different from “3” or “3.1415”?",
         "yesNoAnswer": "Yes",
         "additionalInfo": "The value of pi (π) is the ratio of the circumference to the diameter, with all its infinite decimal digits. Any abbreviated value causes pi (π) to cease being pi (π)."
      }
   ],
   "game_v2": null,
   "date_time": null
}
